OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa|----aaccttiivvaattee yy|nn|aayy
              Change the active state of LVs.  An active LV can be used
              through a block device, allowing data on the LV to be accessed.
              yy makes LVs active, or available.  nn makes LVs inactive, or un-
              available.  The block device for the LV is added or removed from
              the system using device-mapper in the kernel.  A symbolic link
              /dev/VGName/LVName pointing to the device node is also added/re-
              moved.  All software and scripts should access the device
              through the symbolic link and present this as the name of the
              device.  The location and name of the underlying device node may
              depend on the distribution, configuration (e.g. udev), or re-
              lease version.  aayy specifies autoactivation, in which case an LV
              is activated only if it matches an item in lvm.conf activa-
              tion/auto_activation_volume_list.  If the list is not set, all
              LVs are considered to match, and if if the list is set but
              empty, no LVs match.  Autoactivation should be used during sys-
              tem boot to make it possible to select which LVs should be auto-
              matically activated by the system.  See llvvmmlloocckkdd(8) for more in-
              formation about activation options eeyy and ssyy for shared VGs.

       ----aaccttiivvaattiioonnmmooddee ppaarrttiiaall|ddeeggrraaddeedd|ccoommpplleettee
              Determines if LV activation is allowed when PVs are missing,
              e.g. because of a device failure.  ccoommpplleettee only allows LVs with
              no missing PVs to be activated, and is the most restrictive
              mode.  ddeeggrraaddeedd allows RAID LVs with missing PVs to be acti-
              vated.  (This does not include the "mirror" type, see "raid1"
              instead.)  ppaarrttiiaall allows any LV with missing PVs to be acti-
              vated, and should only be used for recovery or repair.  For de-
              fault, see lvm.conf/activation_mode.  See llvvmmrraaiidd(7) for more
              information.

       ----aaddddttaagg _T_a_g
              Adds a tag to a PV, VG or LV. This option can be repeated to add
              multiple tags at once. See llvvmm(8) for information about tags.

       ----aalllloocc ccoonnttiigguuoouuss|cclliinngg|cclliinngg__bbyy__ttaaggss|nnoorrmmaall|aannyywwhheerree|iinnhheerriitt
              Determines the allocation policy when a command needs to allo-
              cate Physical Extents (PEs) from the VG. Each VG and LV has an
              allocation policy which can be changed with vgchange/lvchange,
              or overriden on the command line.  nnoorrmmaall applies common sense
              rules such as not placing parallel stripes on the same PV.  iinn--
              hheerriitt applies the VG policy to an LV.  ccoonnttiigguuoouuss requires new
              PEs be placed adjacent to existing PEs.  cclliinngg places new PEs on
              the same PV as existing PEs in the same stripe of the LV.  If
              there are sufficient PEs for an allocation, but normal does not
              use them, aannyywwhheerree will use them even if it reduces performance,
              e.g. by placing two stripes on the same PV.  Optional positional
              PV args on the command line can also be used to limit which PVs
              the command will use for allocation.  See llvvmm(8) for more infor-
              mation about allocation.

       --AA|----aauuttoobbaacckkuupp yy|nn
              Specifies if metadata should be backed up automatically after a
              change.  Enabling this is strongly advised! See vvggccffggbbaacckkuupp(8)
              for more information.

       ----ccaacchheemmooddee wwrriitteetthhrroouugghh|wwrriitteebbaacckk|ppaasssstthhrroouugghh
              Specifies when writes to a cache LV should be considered com-
              plete.  wwrriitteebbaacckk considers a write complete as soon as it is
              stored in the cache pool.  wwrriitteetthhoouugghh considers a write com-
              plete only when it has been stored in both the cache pool and on
              the origin LV.  While writethrough may be slower for writes, it
              is more resilient if something should happen to a device associ-
              ated with the cache pool LV. With ppaasssstthhrroouugghh, all reads are
              served from the origin LV (all reads miss the cache) and all
              writes are forwarded to the origin LV; additionally, write hits
              cause cache block invalidates. See llvvmmccaacchhee(7) for more informa-
              tion.

       ----ccaacchheeppoolliiccyy _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Specifies the cache policy for a cache LV.  See llvvmmccaacchhee(7) for
              more information.

       ----ccaacchheesseettttiinnggss _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Specifies tunable values for a cache LV in "Key = Value" form.
              Repeat this option to specify multiple values.  (The default
              values should usually be adequate.)  The special string value
              ddeeffaauulltt switches settings back to their default kernel values
              and removes them from the list of settings stored in LVM meta-
              data.  See llvvmmccaacchhee(7) for more information.

       ----ccoommmmaannddpprrooffiillee _S_t_r_i_n_g
              The command profile to use for command configuration.  See
              llvvmm..ccoonnff(5) for more information about profiles.

       ----ccoommpprreessssiioonn yy|nn
              Controls whether compression is enabled or disable for VDO vol-
              ume.  See llvvmmvvddoo(7) for more information about VDO usage.

       ----ccoonnffiigg _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Config settings for the command. These override lvm.conf set-
              tings.  The String arg uses the same format as lvm.conf, or may
              use section/field syntax.  See llvvmm..ccoonnff(5) for more information
              about config.

       --CC|----ccoonnttiigguuoouuss yy|nn
              Sets or resets the contiguous allocation policy for LVs.  De-
              fault is no contiguous allocation based on a next free princi-
              ple.  It is only possible to change a non-contiguous allocation
              policy to contiguous if all of the allocated physical extents in
              the LV are already contiguous.

       --dd|----ddeebbuugg ...
              Set debug level. Repeat from 1 to 6 times to increase the detail
              of messages sent to the log file and/or syslog (if configured).

       ----ddeedduupplliiccaattiioonn yy|nn
              Controls whether deduplication is enabled or disable for VDO
              volume.  See llvvmmvvddoo(7) for more information about VDO usage.

       ----ddeellttaagg _T_a_g
              Deletes a tag from a PV, VG or LV. This option can be repeated
              to delete multiple tags at once. See llvvmm(8) for information
              about tags.

       ----ddeettaacchhpprrooffiillee
              Detaches a metadata profile from a VG or LV.  See llvvmm..ccoonnff(5)
              for more information about profiles.

       ----ddiissccaarrddss ppaassssddoowwnn|nnooppaassssddoowwnn|iiggnnoorree
              Specifies how the device-mapper thin pool layer in the kernel
              should handle discards.  iiggnnoorree causes the thin pool to ignore
              discards.  nnooppaassssddoowwnn causes the thin pool to process discards
              itself to allow reuse of unneeded extents in the thin pool.
              ppaassssddoowwnn causes the thin pool to process discards itself (like
              nopassdown) and pass the discards to the underlying device.  See
              llvvmmtthhiinn(7) for more information.

       ----ddrriivveerrllooaaddeedd yy|nn
              If set to no, the command will not attempt to use device-mapper.
              For testing and debugging.

       ----eerrrroorrwwhheennffuullll yy|nn
              Specifies thin pool behavior when data space is exhausted.  When
              yes, device-mapper will immediately return an error when a thin
              pool is full and an I/O request requires space.  When no, de-
              vice-mapper will queue these I/O requests for a period of time
              to allow the thin pool to be extended.  Errors are returned if
              no space is available after the timeout.  (Also see dm-thin-pool
              kernel module option no_space_timeout.)  See llvvmmtthhiinn(7) for more
              information.

       --ff|----ffoorrccee ...
              Override various checks, confirmations and protections.  Use
              with extreme caution.

       --hh|----hheellpp
              Display help text.

       --KK|----iiggnnoorreeaaccttiivvaattiioonnsskkiipp
              Ignore the "activation skip" LV flag during activation to allow
              LVs with the flag set to be activated.

       ----iiggnnoorreelloocckkiinnggffaaiilluurree
              Allows a command to continue with read-only metadata operations
              after locking failures.

       ----iiggnnoorreemmoonniittoorriinngg
              Do not interact with dmeventd unless --monitor is specified.  Do
              not use this if dmeventd is already monitoring a device.

       ----lloocckkoopptt _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Used to pass options for special cases to lvmlockd.  See llvvmm--
              lloocckkdd(8) for more information.

       ----lloonngghheellpp
              Display long help text.

       --jj|----mmaajjoorr _N_u_m_b_e_r
              Sets the major number of an LV block device.

       ----[[rraaiidd]]mmaaxxrreeccoovveerryyrraattee _S_i_z_e[k|UNIT]
              Sets the maximum recovery rate for a RAID LV.  The rate value is
              an amount of data per second for each device in the array.  Set-
              ting the rate to 0 means it will be unbounded.  See llvvmmrraaiidd(7)
              for more information.

       ----mmeettaaddaattaapprrooffiillee _S_t_r_i_n_g
              The metadata profile to use for command configuration.  See
              llvvmm..ccoonnff(5) for more information about profiles.

       ----mmiinnoorr _N_u_m_b_e_r
              Sets the minor number of an LV block device.

       ----[[rraaiidd]]mmiinnrreeccoovveerryyrraattee _S_i_z_e[k|UNIT]
              Sets the minimum recovery rate for a RAID LV.  The rate value is
              an amount of data per second for each device in the array.  Set-
              ting the rate to 0 means it will be unbounded.  See llvvmmrraaiidd(7)
              for more information.

       ----mmoonniittoorr yy|nn
              Start (yes) or stop (no) monitoring an LV with dmeventd.
              dmeventd monitors kernel events for an LV, and performs auto-
              mated maintenance for the LV in reponse to specific events.  See
              ddmmeevveennttdd(8) for more information.

       ----nnoolloocckkiinngg
              Disable locking.

       ----nnoouuddeevvssyynncc
              Disables udev synchronisation. The process will not wait for no-
              tification from udev. It will continue irrespective of any pos-
              sible udev processing in the background. Only use this if udev
              is not running or has rules that ignore the devices LVM creates.

       --PP|----ppaarrttiiaall
              Commands will do their best to activate LVs with missing PV ex-
              tents.  Missing extents may be replaced with error or zero seg-
              ments according to the lvm.conf missing_stripe_filler setting.
              Metadata may not be changed with this option.

       --pp|----ppeerrmmiissssiioonn rrww|rr
              Set access permission to read only rr or read and write rrww.

       --MM|----ppeerrssiisstteenntt yy|nn
              When yes, makes the specified minor number persistent.

       ----ppoollll yy|nn
              When yes, start the background transformation of an LV.  An in-
              complete transformation, e.g. pvmove or lvconvert interrupted by
              reboot or crash, can be restarted from the last checkpoint with
              --poll y.  When no, background transformation of an LV will not
              occur, and the transformation will not complete. It may not be
              appropriate to immediately poll an LV after activation, in which
              case --poll n can be used to defer polling until a later --poll
              y command.

       ----pprrooffiillee _S_t_r_i_n_g
              An alias for --commandprofile or --metadataprofile, depending on
              the command.

       --qq|----qquuiieett ...
              Suppress output and log messages. Overrides --debug and --ver-
              bose.  Repeat once to also suppress any prompts with answer
              'no'.

       --rr|----rreeaaddaahheeaadd aauuttoo|nnoonnee|_N_u_m_b_e_r
              Sets read ahead sector count of an LV.  aauuttoo is the default
              which allows the kernel to choose a suitable value automati-
              cally.  nnoonnee is equivalent to zero.

       ----rreeaaddoonnllyy
              Run the command in a special read-only mode which will read on-
              disk metadata without needing to take any locks. This can be
              used to peek inside metadata used by a virtual machine image
              while the virtual machine is running. No attempt will be made to
              communicate with the device-mapper kernel driver, so this option
              is unable to report whether or not LVs are actually in use.

       ----rreebbuuiilldd _P_V
              Selects a PV to rebuild in a raid LV. Multiple PVs can be re-
              built by repeating this option.  Use this option in place of
              --resync or --syncaction repair when the PVs with corrupted data
              are known, and their data should be reconstructed rather than
              reconstructing default (rotating) data.  See llvvmmrraaiidd(7) for more
              information.

       ----rreeffrreesshh
              If the LV is active, reload its metadata.  This is not necessary
              in normal operation, but may be useful if something has gone
              wrong, or if some form of manual LV sharing is being used.

       ----rreeppoorrttffoorrmmaatt bbaassiicc|jjssoonn
              Overrides current output format for reports which is defined
              globally by the report/output_format setting in lvm.conf.  bbaassiicc
              is the original format with columns and rows.  If there is more
              than one report per command, each report is prefixed with the
              report name for identification. jjssoonn produces report output in
              JSON format. See llvvmmrreeppoorrtt(7) for more information.

       ----rreessyynncc
              Initiates mirror synchronization. Synchronization generally hap-
              pens automatically, but this option forces it to run.  Also see
              --rebuild to synchronize a specific PV.  During synchronization,
              data is read from the primary mirror device and copied to the
              others. This can take considerable time, during which the LV is
              without a complete redundant copy of the data.  See llvvmmrraaiidd(7)
              for more information.

       --SS|----sseelleecctt _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Select objects for processing and reporting based on specified
              criteria.  The criteria syntax is described by ----sseelleecctt hheellpp and
              llvvmmrreeppoorrtt(7).  For reporting commands, one row is displayed for
              each object matching the criteria.  See ----ooppttiioonnss hheellpp for se-
              lectable object fields.  Rows can be displayed with an addi-
              tional "selected" field (-o selected) showing 1 if the row
              matches the selection and 0 otherwise.  For non-reporting com-
              mands which process LVM entities, the selection is used to
              choose items to process.

       --kk|----sseettaaccttiivvaattiioonnsskkiipp yy|nn
              Persistently sets (yes) or clears (no) the "activation skip"
              flag on an LV.  An LV with this flag set is not activated unless
              the --ignoreactivationskip option is used by the activation com-
              mand.  This flag is set by default on new thin snapshot LVs.
              The flag is not applied to deactivation.  The current value of
              the flag is indicated in the lvs lv_attr bits.

       ----[[rraaiidd]]ssyynnccaaccttiioonn cchheecckk|rreeppaaiirr
              Initiate different types of RAID synchronization.  This causes
              the RAID LV to read all data and parity blocks in the array and
              check for discrepancies (mismatches between mirrors or incorrect
              parity values).  cchheecckk will count but not correct discrepancies.
              rreeppaaiirr will correct discrepancies.  See lvs for reporting dis-
              crepancies found or repaired.

       ----ssyyssiinniitt
              Indicates that vgchange/lvchange is being invoked from early
              system initialisation scripts (e.g. rc.sysinit or an initrd),
              before writable filesystems are available. As such, some func-
              tionality needs to be disabled and this option acts as a short-
              cut which selects an appropriate set of options. Currently, this
              is equivalent to using --ignorelockingfailure, --ignoremonitor-
              ing, --poll n, and setting env var LVM_SUPPRESS_LOCKING_FAIL-
              URE_MESSAGES.  vgchange/lvchange skip autoactivation, and defer
              to pvscan autoactivation.

       --tt|----tteesstt
              Run in test mode. Commands will not update metadata.  This is
              implemented by disabling all metadata writing but nevertheless
              returning success to the calling function. This may lead to un-
              usual error messages in multi-stage operations if a tool relies
              on reading back metadata it believes has changed but hasn't.

       --vv|----vveerrbboossee ...
              Set verbose level. Repeat from 1 to 4 times to increase the de-
              tail of messages sent to stdout and stderr.

       ----vveerrssiioonn
              Display version information.

       ----[[rraaiidd]]wwrriitteebbeehhiinndd _N_u_m_b_e_r
              The maximum number of outstanding writes that are allowed to de-
              vices in a RAID1 LV that is marked write-mostly.  Once this
              value is exceeded, writes become synchronous (i.e. all writes to
              the constituent devices must complete before the array signals
              the write has completed). Setting the value to zero clears the
              preference and allows the system to choose the value arbitrar-
              ily.

       ----[[rraaiidd]]wwrriitteemmoossttllyy _P_V[::tt|nn|yy]
              Mark a device in a RAID1 LV as write-mostly.  All reads to these
              drives will be avoided unless absolutely necessary. This keeps
              the number of I/Os to the drive to a minimum. The default behav-
              ior is to set the write-mostly attribute for the specified PV.
              It is also possible to remove the write-mostly flag by adding
              the suffix ::nn at the end of the PV name, or to toggle the value
              with the suffix ::tt. Repeat this option to change the attribute
              on multiple PVs.

       --yy|----yyeess
              Do not prompt for confirmation interactively but always assume
              the answer yes. Use with extreme caution.  (For automatic no,
              see -qq.)

       --ZZ|----zzeerroo yy|nn
              Set zeroing mode for thin pool. Note: already provisioned blocks
              from pool in non-zero mode are not cleared in unwritten parts
              when setting --zero y.

VVAARRIIAABBLLEESS
       _V_G
              Volume Group name.  See llvvmm(8) for valid names.

       _L_V
              Logical Volume name.  See llvvmm(8) for valid names.  An  LV  posi-
              tional  arg  generally  includes  the  VG name and LV name, e.g.
              VG/LV.  LV followed by _<type> indicates that an LV of the given
              type is required. (raid represents raid<N> type)

       _T_a_g
              Tag  name.  See llvvmm(8) for information about tag names and using
              tags in place of a VG, LV or PV.

       _S_e_l_e_c_t
              Select indicates that a required  positional  parameter  can  be
              omitted  if the ----sseelleecctt option is used.  No arg appears in this
              position.

       _S_t_r_i_n_g
              See the option description for information about the string con-
              tent.

       _S_i_z_e[UNIT]
              Size  is  an  input number that accepts an optional unit.  Input
              units are always treated as base two values, regardless of capi-
              talization,  e.g.  'k'  and 'K' both refer to 1024.  The default
              input unit is specified by letter, followed by |UNIT.  UNIT rep-
              resents  other  possible  input units: bbBBssSSkkKKmmMMggGGttTTppPPeeEE.  b|B is
              bytes, s|S is sectors of 512 bytes, k|K is KiB, m|M is MiB,  g|G
              is GiB, t|T is TiB, p|P is PiB, e|E is EiB.  (This should not be
              confused with the output control --units, where capital  letters
              mean multiple of 1000.)

